WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.871
Since 2024, the Constitution no longer stops a city from enforcing camping bans when shelter is unavailable.

2
00:00:07.871 --> 00:00:15.420
The practical limits now come from state funding rules, property and disability law, and how much shelter a city actually has.

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:29.345
In City of Grants Pass versus Johnson, decided in June 2024 by a six to three vote, the United States Supreme Court held that enforcing general camping laws is not cruel and unusual punishment, even when no shelter is available.

4
00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:34.598
That overturned a Ninth Circuit rule that had shaped West Coast policy for five years.

5
00:00:34.598 --> 00:00:35.697
But limits remain.

6
00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:39.911
Homeless people's belongings are protected from unreasonable seizure.

7
00:00:39.911 --> 00:00:48.462
The Americans with Disabilities Act still applies; one city was allowed to clear an encampment but had to give disabled residents more time.

8
00:00:48.462 --> 00:00:57.440
And a California law that took effect in 2026 bars cities from penalizing anyone for giving homeless people food, water, blankets, or medical care.

9
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.252
The money comes with conditions.

10
00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:10.972
The state's next round of homelessness grants requires a compliant housing element and encampment policies that follow state guidance, which makes housing element compliance a homelessness funding issue too.

11
00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:17.314
In Los Angeles County, Measure A, a half-cent sales tax approved in 2024, raises about a billion dollars a year.

12
00:01:17.314 --> 00:01:28.071
Torrance's share for 2026 and 2027 from its local solutions fund is about five hundred ninety-seven thousand dollars, and that money cannot pay for police or for prosecuting homeless people.

13
00:01:28.071 --> 00:01:30.959
Any enforcement ordinance needs a different source.

14
00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:38.206
In Torrance, the main shelter is a village of forty small cabins that opened in 2022, where the average stay is close to a year.

15
00:01:38.206 --> 00:01:46.982
In September 2025, the council directed staff to draft an ordinance allowing arrest after repeated refusals of shelter, paired with low-cost tent shelters.

16
00:01:46.982 --> 00:01:50.549
Whether it was adopted should be confirmed with the city clerk.

17
00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:57.740
The published counts of how many people are homeless in Torrance conflict, so check the county's own figures before citing one.

18
00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.271
The second half of this episode is about the people who don't come to hearings.

19
00:02:02.271 --> 00:02:09.670
About forty-five percent of Torrance households rent, and forty percent of residents speak a language other than English at home.

20
00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.825
Yet the people who testify are mostly older homeowners.

21
00:02:12.825 --> 00:02:16.495
Good engagement closes that gap before an item reaches the dais.

22
00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:19.822
Start by saying what level of engagement you are offering.

23
00:02:19.822 --> 00:02:25.902
Informing people is different from consulting them, and consulting is different from sharing the decision.

24
00:02:25.902 --> 00:02:32.957
Most hearings consult, which means the public is owed a summary afterward of what they said and what changed because of it.

25
00:02:32.957 --> 00:02:39.552
Then go where people already are: school pickup lines, apartment complexes, the transit center, cultural festivals.

26
00:02:39.552 --> 00:02:43.911
Partner with groups residents already trust, like congregations and schools.

27
00:02:43.911 --> 00:02:47.525
Offer childcare, food, interpretation, and sometimes a stipend.

28
00:02:47.525 --> 00:02:51.368
Use randomly selected panels or valid surveys to balance testimony.

29
00:02:51.368 --> 00:02:59.283
Petaluma did this in 2022, paying thirty-six randomly chosen residents to spend about ninety hours deciding the future of its fairgrounds.

30
00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:04.387
Vallejo let residents as young as sixteen vote on how to spend millions in capital funds.

31
00:03:04.387 --> 00:03:11.213
And Torrance Unified's phone policy survey drew more than four thousand responses, far more than any hearing ever will.

32
00:03:11.213 --> 00:03:16.260
For more, see the homelessness and engagement sections of the Local Official's Handbook.
